//! Whites and Blacks as Adobe Camera Raw (process version 2012) applies them.
//!
//! Measured as a black box on the Camera Raw Filter of Photoshop 25.4: grey blocks with chosen
//! histograms went through the filter by script and came back exactly; no Adobe code or data
//! was used.
//!
//! - Both are global curves of the grey level, applied after Shadows and Highlights, Whites
//!   first. On an image whose darkest and lightest percent reach black and white the curve of a
//!   slider value `x` is the measured row at `x` of [`WHITES`] or [`BLACKS`].
//! - The direction that only compresses (Whites down, Blacks up) is that fixed curve on every
//!   image. The direction that clips (Whites up, Blacks down) adapts: the row used is
//!   `x · A(BP, WP)`. On the block images BP and WP are their darkest and lightest levels; on
//!   photographs Photoshop's amounts follow best with BP the 3rd percentile of the luminance and
//!   WP the 99.5th of the brightest channel, and Blacks at least 1.2 times. A low
//!   white point makes Whites +100 stronger (up to 2.7 times), a raised black point Blacks
//!   (up to 1.45 times at −40). Whites rows past +100 come from images where `A > 1`.
//! - Not reproduced: past −40 Photoshop's Blacks grows faster on images with a raised black
//!   point (on one spanning 0.6–1 its −100 blacks out everything below 0.74, here below about
//!   0.15); its curves there are not rows of the full-range family.
//! - Colour: the clipping directions act on each channel, the compressing ones scale the pixel
//!   by the curve's change of its luminance (closest to Photoshop on saturated colours).

